INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','easy','mcq','Aspirin irreversibly inhibits which cyclooxygenase enzyme, and by what chemical mechanism?','["A. COX-1, by irreversible acetylation of a serine residue at the active site", "B. COX-2, by reversible competitive inhibition at the catalytic site", "C. Both COX-1 and COX-2 equally, by covalent binding to a tyrosine residue", "D. COX-2 selectively, by acetylation preventing arachidonic acid binding"]','A','Aspirin irreversibly acetylates a serine residue on the active site of COX-1, permanently inactivating the enzyme. Other NSAIDs are reversible inhibitors. This irreversible inhibition lasts for the lifetime of the platelet (7-10 days), explaining aspirin''''s sustained antiplatelet effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with coronary artery disease and prior gastric ulcer requires NSAID therapy for osteoarthritis. Which regimen is most appropriate?','["A. Celecoxib alone", "B. Naproxen with omeprazole", "C. Indomethacin with misoprostol", "D. Ibuprofen alone"]','B','This patient has significant cardiovascular risk, making celecoxib (selective COX-2 inhibitor) a poor choice due to increased cardiovascular events. Naproxen is considered the safest NSAID regarding cardiovascular disease. Addition of omeprazole (PPI) provides gastroprotection for this high GI-risk patient.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','medium','mcq','COX-2 is constitutively expressed in the brain, kidney, and bone. Its expression at other sites is upregulated by which inflammatory mediators?','["A. Prostaglandins E2 and I2", "B. Thromboxane A2 and PAF", "C. TNF-alpha and IL-1", "D. Bradykinin and histamine"]','C','COX-2 is constitutively expressed in tissues such as the brain, kidney, and bone. Its expression at other sites is increased by inflammatory mediators like TNF-alpha and IL-1, but can be pharmacologically inhibited by glucocorticoids.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','hard','mcq','Selective COX-2 inhibitors increase cardiovascular risk. Which mechanism best explains this?','["A. Increased TXA2 production from COX-1 causing vasoconstriction", "B. Direct cardiotoxic effect on myocardial ion channels", "C. Inhibition of COX-1 platelet function causing compensatory thrombosis", "D. Decreased prostacyclin (PGI2) production with relative excess of thromboxane A2 (TXA2) promoting prothrombotic state"]','D','COX-2 produces prostacyclin (PGI2) in endothelial cells, which causes vasodilation and inhibits platelet aggregation. Selective COX-2 inhibitors reduce PGI2 while COX-1-mediated TXA2 (vasoconstriction/platelet aggregation) remains intact, creating a prothrombotic imbalance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','medium','mcq','What is the primary mechanism by which NSAIDs cause renal toxicity?','["A. Inhibition of PGE2 and PGI2 synthesis reducing renal blood flow and causing sodium/water retention", "B. Direct tubular toxicity from the parent compound", "C. Immune-mediated glomerulonephritis via hapten formation", "D. Accumulation of toxic metabolites in the renal cortex"]','A','NSAIDs prevent synthesis of PGE2 and PGI2, prostaglandins that maintain renal blood flow especially in states of volume depletion or renal impairment. Their inhibition causes sodium and water retention and can lead to acute kidney injury in susceptible patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','easy','mcq','Which NSAID is available as a topical gel for osteoarthritis of the knee?','["A. Naproxen", "B. Diclofenac", "C. Indomethacin", "D. Piroxicam"]','B','Diclofenac is available in topical formulations (gel or solution) for treatment of osteoarthritis in the knees or hands, offering local anti-inflammatory activity with reduced systemic adverse effects compared to oral NSAIDs.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','hard','mcq','A patient on high-dose aspirin for RA presents with tinnitus, dizziness, and hyperventilation. Which pharmacokinetic change explains increased toxicity at anti-inflammatory doses?','["A. Induction of CYP2C9 leading to faster aspirin metabolism", "B. Decreased protein binding due to competition with salicylate", "C. Saturation of hepatic conjugation pathways causing zero-order kinetics and prolonged half-life", "D. Increased renal tubular secretion of salicylate"]','C','At anti-inflammatory doses of aspirin (>4 g/day), hepatic metabolic pathways become saturated, resulting in zero-order kinetics. The half-life increases from 3.5 hours to 15 hours or more, leading to salicylate accumulation and salicylism (tinnitus, dizziness, hyperventilation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','medium','mcq','Prostaglandins normally protect the gastric mucosa. Which of the following correctly describes their gastroprotective role?','["A. TXA2 promotes gastric mucosal healing by increasing blood flow", "B. LTB4 maintains tight junctions in the gastric epithelium", "C. PGD2 increases bicarbonate secretion in the duodenum to neutralise acid", "D. PGI2 inhibits gastric acid secretion; PGE2 and PGF2alpha stimulate synthesis of protective mucus"]','D','PGI2 inhibits gastric acid secretion, while PGE2 and PGF2alpha stimulate synthesis of protective mucus in the stomach and small intestine. NSAIDs inhibiting COX-1 reduce these beneficial prostaglandins, increasing gastric acid secretion, diminishing mucus protection, and increasing risk of GI bleeding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','easy','mcq','Why should aspirin be avoided in children with chickenpox or influenza?','["A. Risk of Reye syndrome \u2014 fulminating hepatitis with cerebral edema, often fatal", "B. Risk of nephrotoxicity from aspirin metabolites in immature kidneys", "C. Aspirin inhibits viral clearance by suppressing the immune response", "D. Risk of aplastic anaemia in paediatric patients"]','A','Aspirin should be avoided in patients younger than 19 years with viral infections to prevent Reye syndrome, a syndrome that can cause fulminating hepatitis with cerebral edema, often leading to death. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen are preferred.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','NSAIDs','hard','mcq','Celecoxib is metabolised primarily by CYP2C9. What is the clinical consequence of co-administering fluconazole (a CYP2C9 inhibitor) with celecoxib?','["A. Fluconazole induces CYP3A4 decreasing clearance of statins co-prescribed with celecoxib", "B. Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9 increasing celecoxib serum levels and risk of adverse effects", "C. Fluconazole inhibits CYP2D6 increasing risk of opioid toxicity from concurrent opioids", "D. Fluconazole reduces anti-inflammatory efficacy by competing for COX-2 binding"]','B','Celecoxib is extensively metabolised by CYP2C9. Inhibitors of CYP2C9 such as fluconazole may increase serum levels of celecoxib, potentially increasing adverse effects. The dosage should be reduced in patients with moderate hepatic impairment.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','easy','mcq','What metabolite of paracetamol (acetaminophen) causes hepatic necrosis in overdose, and what depletes the protective mechanism?','["A. Sulfate conjugate saturates hepatic enzyme systems causing hepatocyte death", "B. Glucuronide metabolite accumulates in bile causing cholestatic hepatitis", "C. NAPQI depletes glutathione, then reacts with hepatic proteins causing necrosis", "D. Mercapturic acid directly alkylates hepatocyte DNA"]','C','In overdose, hepatic glutathione becomes depleted. The highly reactive metabolite NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinoneimine) then reacts with sulfhydryl groups of hepatic proteins, causing hepatic necrosis. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione and is the antidote.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','medium','mcq','Which opioid is absolutely contraindicated in patients with renal insufficiency due to accumulation of a neurotoxic metabolite causing seizures?','["A. Morphine (M6G accumulates causing excessive sedation)", "B. Tramadol (O-desmethyltramadol accumulates causing respiratory depression)", "C. Codeine (accumulation of morphine via CYP2D6)", "D. Meperidine (normeperidine accumulates causing delirium, myoclonus, and seizures)"]','D','Meperidine is absolutely contraindicated in renal insufficiency. Its active metabolite normeperidine is potentially neurotoxic. In patients with renal insufficiency, normeperidine accumulates leading to delirium, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and seizures — effects that are NOT reversed by naloxone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','medium','mcq','Which opioid has dual mechanisms — mu-opioid receptor agonism AND norepinephrine reuptake inhibition — making it effective for both nociceptive and neuropathic pain?','["A. Tapentadol", "B. Oxycodone", "C. Tramadol", "D. Buprenorphine"]','A','Tapentadol has a dual mechanism of action: mu opioid receptor agonism AND inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake. This combination is effective for both nociceptive and neuropathic pain, including diabetic peripheral neuropathy and chronic back pain with radiculopathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','hard','mcq','Codeine requires conversion to morphine by CYP2D6 for analgesic effect. What is the risk for CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolisers given codeine?','["A. Reduced analgesia due to faster codeine elimination before conversion", "B. Life-threatening opioid toxicity from excess morphine production (respiratory depression and death)", "C. Serotonin syndrome from excessive norepinephrine reuptake inhibition", "D. Increased constipation from accumulation of unconverted codeine"]','B','CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolisers convert more codeine to morphine than expected, resulting in dangerously high morphine plasma levels causing life-threatening respiratory depression and death. Life-threatening events have been reported in children receiving codeine post-tonsillectomy. An FDA black box warning was issued.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','easy','mcq','What is the specific antidote for paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose?','["A. Naloxone", "B. Flumazenil", "C. N-acetylcysteine", "D. Atropine"]','C','N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for acetaminophen overdose. It replenishes hepatic glutathione stores, allowing the toxic metabolite NAPQI to be safely conjugated rather than causing hepatic necrosis. It is most effective when given within 8-10 hours of overdose.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','medium','mcq','A patient on long-term opioid therapy for chronic back pain develops tolerance to analgesia. Which adverse effect shows LEAST tolerance and will most likely persist?','["A. Sedation", "B. Respiratory depression", "C. Nausea and emesis", "D. Constipation"]','D','Tolerance develops to most opioid effects including sedation, respiratory depression, euphoria, and nausea. However, tolerance does NOT develop significantly to opioid-induced constipation and miosis (pupillary constriction). These effects persist throughout long-term opioid therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','hard','mcq','Tramadol carries a risk of serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs or MAOIs. Additionally, naloxone only partially reverses tramadol toxicity and may increase the risk of which specific adverse effect?','["A. Seizures", "B. Respiratory depression", "C. QTc prolongation", "D. Hepatotoxicity"]','A','Tramadol toxicity manifested by CNS excitation and seizures can occur with overdose or drug interactions with SSRIs, MAOIs, and tricyclic antidepressants. Administration of naloxone can only partially reverse tramadol toxicity and has been associated with an increased risk of seizures by removing the opioid-mediated seizure threshold elevation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','medium','mcq','Which statement correctly describes fentanyl transdermal patch use for chronic severe MSK pain?','["A. It is appropriate for opioid-naive patients with acute breakthrough pain", "B. It is contraindicated in opioid-naive patients and should not be used for acute pain", "C. Onset of analgesia occurs within 30 minutes of application", "D. It should be changed every 24 hours for optimal drug delivery"]','B','The fentanyl transdermal patch has a delayed onset of at least 12 hours and a prolonged offset. It is contraindicated in opioid-naive patients and should NOT be used for acute or postoperative pain. It is designed for management of chronic severe pain in opioid-tolerant patients and is changed every 72 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','easy','mcq','Which opioid is a partial mu-receptor agonist with a ceiling effect, resulting in lower risk of fatal respiratory depression compared with full agonists?','["A. Morphine", "B. Oxycodone", "C. Buprenorphine", "D. Fentanyl"]','C','Buprenorphine is a potent partial agonist at the mu receptor and an antagonist at kappa receptors. Due to its partial mu agonist activity, there is a ceiling effect causing less euphoric effects, lower abuse potential, and reduced risk of respiratory depression compared with full mu agonists.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Analgesics in MSK','hard','mcq','Methadone has unique pharmacological properties. Which correctly describes its mechanism and the key clinical challenge with its use?','["A. Methadone is a kappa agonist with short half-life; it is safe for all patients including those with hepatic disease", "B. Methadone is a pure mu agonist with no NMDA activity; its clinical challenge is its very short half-life requiring dosing every 2 hours", "C. Methadone induces CYP3A4 reducing efficacy of all co-prescribed opioids", "D. Methadone is a mu agonist, NMDA receptor antagonist, and norepinephrine/serotonin reuptake inhibitor; its long variable half-life (12-150 hours) with shorter duration of analgesia (4-8 hours) risks accumulation and QTc prolongation"]','D','Methadone is a mu agonist, NMDA receptor antagonist, and norepinephrine/serotonin reuptake inhibitor. Despite its long half-life (12-150 hours), the duration of analgesia is only 4-8 hours, creating risk of accumulation and overdose. Methadone also prolongs QTc and can cause torsades de pointes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','easy','mcq','Methotrexate is the preferred first-line DMARD for RA. What is its primary mechanism of action?','["A. Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase blocking pyrimidine synthesis", "B. Folic acid antagonism inhibiting cytokine production and purine nucleotide biosynthesis", "C. Blocking CD20 on B lymphocytes leading to B-cell depletion", "D. Competitive inhibition of xanthine oxidase"]','B','Methotrexate is a folic acid antagonist that inhibits cytokine production and purine nucleotide biosynthesis, leading to immunosuppressive and anti-inflammatory effects. It is administered once weekly at doses much lower than those used in cancer chemotherapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','medium','mcq','A patient with RA on methotrexate develops mouth ulcers and nausea. Which simple intervention reduces these adverse effects without compromising efficacy?','["A. Add folic acid supplementation", "B. Switch to weekly intramuscular injections", "C. Reduce dosing frequency to fortnightly", "D. Discontinue methotrexate and switch to leflunomide immediately"]','A','Supplementation with folic acid may improve tolerability of methotrexate and reduce GI and hepatic adverse effects, including mucosal ulceration and nausea, without significantly reducing anti-inflammatory efficacy. This is standard clinical practice in RA management.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','medium','mcq','Hydroxychloroquine is used for early mild RA. Which serious adverse effect requires regular ophthalmological monitoring?','["A. Hepatotoxicity requiring liver biopsy", "B. Leukopenia requiring frequent CBC monitoring", "C. Nephrotoxicity requiring renal function tests every 3 months", "D. Irreversible retinal damage (maculopathy) and corneal deposits"]','D','Hydroxychloroquine may cause ocular toxicity, including irreversible retinal damage (maculopathy) and corneal deposits requiring regular ophthalmological monitoring. It has less adverse effects on the liver and immune system than other DMARDs. Onset of effects takes 6 weeks to 6 months.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','hard','mcq','Leflunomide is an immunomodulatory DMARD for RA. What is its precise mechanism?','["A. Alkylation of DNA in rapidly dividing autoimmune lymphocytes", "B. Inhibition of JAK1/JAK3 signalling pathways downstream of cytokine receptors", "C. Reversible inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (DHODH) blocking pyrimidine synthesis in proliferating lymphocytes", "D. Competitive inhibition of thymidylate synthase reducing DNA precursor availability"]','C','Leflunomide preferentially causes cell arrest of autoimmune lymphocytes through its active metabolite''''s action on dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (DHODH). DHODH is necessary for de novo pyrimidine synthesis in proliferating cells including activated lymphocytes. It is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','easy','mcq','Which traditional DMARD is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy due to teratogenicity?','["A. Hydroxychloroquine", "B. Methotrexate", "C. Sulfasalazine", "D. Low-dose prednisolone"]','B','Methotrexate is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy due to its teratogenic and abortifacient effects. Leflunomide is also absolutely contraindicated. Any woman of childbearing potential on methotrexate must use effective contraception and be counselled about these risks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','medium','mcq','What is the mechanism of action and key adverse effect of sulfasalazine in RA?','["A. Mechanism unknown in RA; associated with GI effects (nausea, vomiting) and leukopenia, with onset 1-3 months", "B. Inhibits folate metabolism; causes reversible retinal toxicity requiring monitoring", "C. Blocks dihydroorotate dehydrogenase; causes hepatotoxicity requiring liver biopsy", "D. Inhibits xanthine oxidase; causes hyperuricaemia and gout exacerbation"]','A','Sulfasalazine''''s mechanism of action in treating RA is unclear. The onset of activity is 1 to 3 months, and it is associated with GI adverse effects (nausea, vomiting, anorexia) and leukopenia. Its use in RA is similar to leflunomide.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','hard','mcq','A patient with RA has inadequate response to methotrexate monotherapy after 3 months. What is the recommended next strategy?','["A. Replace methotrexate with hydroxychloroquine monotherapy", "B. Discontinue all DMARDs and initiate high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "C. Switch to NSAIDs only and await spontaneous remission", "D. Add a TNF-alpha inhibitor or another traditional DMARD to methotrexate (combination therapy)"]','D','For patients with inadequate response to methotrexate monotherapy, the recommended strategy is to add another traditional DMARD (combination) or to use a TNF inhibitor or non-TNF biologic agent. Methotrexate should generally be continued as part of the regimen when adding biologics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','medium','mcq','What is the approximate time to clinical response after initiating methotrexate for RA, and what is used as bridge therapy during this period?','["A. 24-48 hours; no bridging therapy needed", "B. 6-12 months; high-dose DMARDs used as bridging therapy", "C. 3-6 weeks; NSAIDs or glucocorticoids are used as bridging therapy", "D. 2-3 years; biological agents used as bridging therapy"]','C','Response to methotrexate usually occurs within 3 to 6 weeks of starting treatment. NSAIDs or glucocorticoids are used as bridging therapy to provide symptomatic relief during this period. Glucocorticoids should be used at the lowest effective dose for the shortest possible duration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','easy','mcq','Which monitoring parameters are recommended during long-term methotrexate therapy?','["A. Monthly echocardiogram and lipid panel", "B. Periodic liver function tests, complete blood counts, and monitoring for signs of infection", "C. Annual bone density scan and ophthalmology review", "D. Monthly thyroid function tests and renal function tests"]','B','Recommended monitoring during methotrexate therapy includes periodic liver function tests (cirrhosis risk), complete blood counts (cytopenias especially leukopenia), and monitoring for signs of infection. Folic acid supplementation is also recommended to reduce adverse effects.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','hard','mcq','A female patient with RA on leflunomide wishes to become pregnant. What must be done before attempting conception?','["A. Cholestyramine washout protocol to accelerate elimination of long-half-life active metabolite; verify plasma levels below threshold before conception", "B. Simply stop leflunomide 3 months before attempting conception", "C. No special precautions needed as leflunomide is safe in the first trimester", "D. Switch to low-dose methotrexate which is safer in pregnancy"]','A','Leflunomide is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy. Leflunomide has a very long-half-life active metabolite (A77 1726) that can persist in plasma for up to 2 years. Before attempting conception, a cholestyramine washout protocol must be performed to accelerate elimination, followed by verification that plasma levels are below the threshold.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','DMARDs for RA','medium','mcq','In patients with inadequate response to one TNF-alpha inhibitor, what are the recommended options according to RA treatment guidelines?','["A. Discontinue all biologic therapy permanently and increase NSAID dose", "B. Only pegloticase is approved for patients failing TNF inhibitors", "C. Repeat the same TNF-alpha inhibitor at double the dose", "D. Add a traditional DMARD, switch to a different TNF-alpha inhibitor, or switch to a non-TNF biologic agent"]','D','If a patient has failed monotherapy with one TNF-alpha inhibitor, a traditional DMARD may be added, or therapy with a non-TNF biologic agent or a different TNF-alpha inhibitor may be tried. TNF-alpha inhibitors and non-TNF biologics should never be used together.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','hard','mcq','Which biologic DMARD is a recombinant fusion protein that competes with CD28 for binding to CD80/CD86 on antigen-presenting cells, preventing T-cell activation?','["A. Adalimumab", "B. Rituximab", "C. Abatacept", "D. Tocilizumab"]','C','Abatacept is a recombinant fusion protein and costimulation modulator that competes with CD28 for binding on CD80/CD86 protein on antigen-presenting cells, preventing full T-cell activation and reducing the inflammatory response. It is administered IV every 4 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','medium','mcq','Before initiating any biologic DMARD for RA, which screening is mandatory due to risk of reactivation?','["A. Hepatitis C antibody screening \u2014 biologics increase HCV replication", "B. Tuberculosis (TB) screening \u2014 biologic therapy can reactivate latent TB", "C. HIV viral load testing \u2014 biologics cause CD4 T-cell depletion", "D. Bone mineral density assessment \u2014 biologics cause rapid bone loss"]','B','Patients receiving biologic DMARDs are at increased risk for tuberculosis reactivation, fungal opportunistic infections, and sepsis. Screening for latent TB before initiating any biologic DMARD is mandatory. Reactivation of hepatitis B may also occur and should be screened for.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','easy','mcq','Adalimumab is classified as which type of biologic agent?','["A. Recombinant human monoclonal antibody targeting TNF-alpha", "B. IL-6 receptor antagonist blocking IL-6 mediated signalling", "C. Anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody depleting B lymphocytes", "D. Fusion protein competing with CD28 on T cells"]','A','Adalimumab is a recombinant human monoclonal antibody that binds to TNF-alpha and interferes with its activity by blocking interaction of TNF-alpha with cell surface receptors. It is administered subcutaneously weekly or every other week.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','medium','mcq','Rituximab depletes B lymphocytes in RA. What is its specific target antigen and how frequently is it administered?','["A. CD4 antigen on T lymphocytes; subcutaneous injection every 2 weeks", "B. TNF-alpha; intramuscular injection monthly", "C. IL-6 receptor; intravenous infusion every 4 weeks", "D. CD20 antigen on B lymphocytes; intravenous infusion every 16-24 weeks"]','D','Rituximab is a chimeric murine/human monoclonal antibody directed against the CD20 antigen on the surface of B lymphocytes. It is administered as an intravenous infusion every 16 to 24 weeks. Prior to infusion, methylprednisolone, acetaminophen, and antihistamine are given to reduce infusion reactions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','hard','mcq','Why should TNF-alpha inhibitors be avoided or used with extreme caution in patients with pre-existing heart failure?','["A. They cause hypertension by increasing angiotensin II production", "B. They reduce cardiac output by blocking beta-adrenergic receptors in cardiomyocytes", "C. They can cause and/or worsen preexisting heart failure", "D. They cause fluid retention through aldosterone-mediated sodium retention"]','C','TNF-alpha inhibitors should be used cautiously in those with heart failure, as they can cause and/or worsen preexisting heart failure. TNF-alpha plays a complex role in cardiac physiology. An increased risk of lymphoma and other malignancies has also been observed with TNF-alpha inhibitor use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','medium','mcq','How does etanercept differ structurally from adalimumab?','["A. Etanercept is a fully human monoclonal antibody; adalimumab is a chimeric mouse-human antibody", "B. Etanercept is a genetically engineered fusion protein (soluble TNF receptor + IgG Fc) binding TNF-alpha; adalimumab is a recombinant monoclonal antibody", "C. Etanercept inhibits IL-6; adalimumab inhibits TNF-alpha", "D. Etanercept is given IV; adalimumab is subcutaneous"]','B','Etanercept is a genetically engineered fusion protein (soluble TNF receptor component fused with IgG1 Fc domain) that binds to TNF-alpha, blocking its interaction with cell surface TNF-alpha receptors. Adalimumab is a recombinant monoclonal antibody directly targeting TNF-alpha. Both are given subcutaneously.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','easy','mcq','Which statement about biologic DMARDs and live vaccinations is correct?','["A. Live vaccinations should NOT be administered to patients taking biologic DMARDs", "B. Live vaccines should be given at any time as biologics do not impair immune response to attenuated organisms", "C. Live vaccines are safe because biologics only suppress the innate immune system", "D. All vaccines including live vaccines should be given every 6 months during biologic therapy"]','A','Live vaccinations should not be administered to patients taking any of the biologic DMARDs due to risk of infection from live attenuated organisms in the immunosuppressed state. All vaccines should ideally be given before starting biologic therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','medium','mcq','Tocilizumab and sarilumab target which inflammatory mediator in RA?','["A. TNF-alpha directly \u2014 blocking its interaction with cell surface receptors", "B. CD20 on B lymphocytes \u2014 causing B-cell depletion", "C. CD80/CD86 on antigen-presenting cells \u2014 preventing T-cell costimulation", "D. IL-6 receptor \u2014 binding inhibits the proinflammatory cytokine IL-6"]','D','Tocilizumab and sarilumab are recombinant monoclonal antibodies that bind to IL-6 receptors and inhibit activity of the proinflammatory cytokine IL-6. Both can be administered subcutaneously every 2 weeks; tocilizumab may also be given intravenously every 4 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','easy','mcq','Which combination of biologic agents must be AVOIDED due to risk of severe infections?','["A. Methotrexate combined with adalimumab", "B. Etanercept combined with hydroxychloroquine", "C. TNF-alpha inhibitor combined with a non-TNF biologic agent (e.g., abatacept + adalimumab)", "D. Rituximab combined with methotrexate"]','C','TNF-alpha inhibitors and non-TNF biologic agents should not be used together due to risk of severe infections from combined excessive immunosuppression. The combination of a TNF inhibitor with a traditional DMARD like methotrexate is appropriate and often recommended.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Biologic Therapies','hard','mcq','Infliximab requires mandatory co-administration with methotrexate. What is the pharmacological basis for this requirement?','["A. Monotherapy with infliximab leads to development of anti-infliximab antibodies (immunogenicity) and reduced efficacy", "B. Infliximab has no efficacy as monotherapy for any indication", "C. Infliximab causes severe hepatotoxicity when used alone without methotrexate''''s hepatoprotective effects", "D. Infliximab is only effective for seronegative RA and requires methotrexate for seropositive disease"]','A','Infliximab is not indicated for monotherapy, as this leads to the development of anti-infliximab antibodies (immunogenicity) and reduced efficacy over time. Methotrexate reduces antibody formation against infliximab. Infliximab is also the only anti-TNF agent given as an IV infusion every 8 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','easy','mcq','Which is the most common adverse effect of long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy in patients with RA?','["A. Hypoglycaemia \u2014 from insulin hypersensitivity", "B. Hyperkalemia \u2014 from mineralocorticoid effects", "C. Bradycardia \u2014 from reduced cardiac sympathetic activity", "D. Osteoporosis \u2014 through inhibition of calcium absorption and bone formation"]','D','Osteoporosis is the most common adverse effect of long-term corticosteroid therapy. Glucocorticoids suppress intestinal calcium absorption, inhibit bone formation (osteoblast activity), and decrease sex hormone synthesis. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation and bisphosphonates may be required.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','hard','mcq','A patient with RA has been on prednisone 10 mg/day for 6 months. How should this be discontinued?','["A. Abrupt discontinuation is safe if disease is well controlled for 3 months", "B. Convert to once-weekly oral dosing for 1 month then stop", "C. Dose must be tapered slowly \u2014 abrupt discontinuation risks acute adrenal insufficiency (HPA axis suppression)", "D. Switch to dexamethasone equivalently then immediately discontinue"]','C','Sudden discontinuation of corticosteroids after prolonged therapy can cause acute adrenal insufficiency due to HPA axis suppression, which can be fatal. The dose must be tapered slowly according to individual tolerance. The risk of exacerbating the underlying disease also supports gradual tapering.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','medium','mcq','What mechanism explains glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis?','["A. Increased osteoclast apoptosis paradoxically reducing bone density", "B. Inhibition of intestinal calcium absorption and inhibition of osteoblast-mediated bone formation", "C. Decreased PTH secretion leading to hypercalciuria", "D. Reduced renal 1-alpha hydroxylase activity reducing active vitamin D"]','B','Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis is attributed primarily to inhibition of intestinal calcium absorption and inhibition of osteoblast-mediated bone formation. Glucocorticoids also decrease sex hormone synthesis. Calcium, vitamin D, and bisphosphonates may be required for prevention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','hard','mcq','Which patient scenario carries the HIGHEST risk of HPA axis suppression requiring a slow corticosteroid taper?','["A. A patient taking prednisone 10 mg daily for 6 months for RA", "B. A patient taking prednisone 40 mg daily for 5 days for an asthma exacerbation", "C. A patient using intranasal mometasone daily for 12 months for allergic rhinitis", "D. A patient receiving one intra-articular methylprednisolone injection for knee osteoarthritis"]','A','HPA axis suppression occurs with higher doses for durations >2 weeks. While the asthma dose is higher, the 5-day duration is insufficient for significant suppression. Topical intranasal steroids and single intra-articular injections pose very low systemic risk. The patient on prednisone 10 mg for 6 months has the highest HPA suppression risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','easy','mcq','Which dosing strategy minimises HPA axis suppression while maintaining efficacy during long-term corticosteroid therapy?','["A. Increasing the total daily dose to shorten overall treatment duration", "B. Giving the entire dose at night to suppress morning cortisol production", "C. Combining with fludrocortisone to maintain mineralocorticoid balance", "D. Alternate-day dosing \u2014 allows HPA axis to recover on steroid-free days"]','D','Alternate-day administration of corticosteroids minimises HPA axis suppression while maintaining therapeutic efficacy, by allowing the HPA axis to recover on the day when no steroid is administered. The antianxiety effects of the underlying disease must still be managed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','hard','mcq','Which metabolic effects of glucocorticoids contribute to iatrogenic hyperglycaemia?','["A. Decreased glucagon release and increased insulin sensitivity", "B. Inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis and increased glucose uptake in peripheral tissues", "C. Stimulation of hepatic gluconeogenesis, decreased peripheral glucose utilisation, and anti-insulin effects", "D. Increased renal glucose threshold reducing urinary glucose excretion"]','C','Corticosteroids cause hyperglycaemia by stimulating hepatic gluconeogenesis, decreasing peripheral glucose utilisation (anti-insulin effect), and promoting insulin resistance. Diabetic patients on corticosteroids require more intensive blood glucose monitoring and medication adjustment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','medium','mcq','Intra-articular corticosteroid injection is most appropriate in acute gout under which circumstance?','["A. More than 6 joints are simultaneously involved in a polyarticular flare", "B. Only one or two joints are affected and oral therapy is contraindicated or impractical", "C. Serum urate levels are above 10 mg/dL requiring urgent reduction", "D. The patient has previously failed colchicine and NSAIDs"]','B','Intra-articular administration of corticosteroids is appropriate in acute gout when only one or two joints are affected. Systemic corticosteroids are used for more widespread joint involvement. This route minimises systemic adverse effects of steroid therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','medium','mcq','The classic Cushing-like syndrome from long-term corticosteroid excess includes which constellation of features?','["A. Redistribution of body fat (moon face, buffalo hump), hirsutism, hyperglycaemia, and osteoporosis", "B. Weight loss, hypotension, hyperpigmentation, and hypoglycaemia", "C. Hypokalemia, hyponatraemia, and muscle wasting exclusively", "D. Cataracts in isolation without systemic metabolic effects"]','A','The classic Cushing-like syndrome from long-term corticosteroid excess includes redistribution of body fat (buffalo hump, central adiposity), puffy moon face, hirsutism, increased appetite, hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, and cataracts. These effects are dose-related.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','easy','mcq','Why are corticosteroids used as bridge therapy in RA?','["A. They permanently replace DMARD therapy in elderly patients unable to tolerate methotrexate", "B. They are exclusively for patients awaiting joint replacement surgery", "C. They prevent the transition from early to established RA by halting disease progression", "D. They provide rapid symptomatic relief while waiting for slower-acting DMARDs (onset 3-6 weeks) to become effective"]','D','Glucocorticoids are potent anti-inflammatory drugs commonly used in RA to provide rapid symptomatic relief and bridge the time until other DMARDs become effective. They should be used at the lowest dose for the shortest possible duration to avoid cumulative adverse effects.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Corticosteroids in MSK','hard','mcq','Which corticosteroid is recommended for fetal lung maturation in preterm labour, and why is prednisone not suitable?','["A. Methylprednisolone \u2014 lower cost and oral availability with adequate fetal penetration", "B. Hydrocortisone \u2014 physiological replacement dose mimics fetal cortisol production", "C. Betamethasone \u2014 high glucocorticoid activity and not inactivated by placental 11-beta-HSD, allowing effective fetal exposure", "D. Fludrocortisone \u2014 highest mineralocorticoid potency optimises fetal lung fluid balance"]','C','Betamethasone and dexamethasone are recommended for fetal lung maturation as they have high glucocorticoid activity and are not inactivated by placental 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, allowing effective fetal exposure. Prednisone is not suitable because the fetus cannot convert it to the active form prednisolone.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','What is the mechanism by which colchicine alleviates acute gout?','["A. Inhibits xanthine oxidase reducing uric acid synthesis acutely", "B. Binds tubulin causing microtubule depolymerisation, impairing neutrophil migration into inflamed joints", "C. Blocks IL-1 receptor preventing crystal-induced cytokine cascade", "D. Increases renal uric acid excretion reducing synovial crystal burden"]','B','Colchicine binds to tubulin causing microtubule depolymerisation. This disrupts cellular functions including neutrophil mobility, decreasing their migration into the inflamed joint. It also blocks cell division by binding to mitotic spindles. It is neither a uricosuric nor an analgesic agent.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','For colchicine to be effective in acute gout, it must be administered within what time frame?','["A. Within 36 hours of attack onset", "B. Within 12 hours of attack onset", "C. Within 24 hours of attack onset", "D. Within 72 hours of attack onset"]','A','Colchicine must be administered within 36 hours of onset of an acute gout attack to be effective, usually alleviating pain within 12 hours. NSAIDs have largely replaced colchicine for safety reasons in acute attacks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','What is the mechanism of action of allopurinol in reducing serum uric acid levels?','["A. Increased renal tubular secretion of uric acid via URAT1 inhibition", "B. Conversion of uric acid to allantoin via recombinant uricase", "C. Blocking intestinal absorption of purines from food", "D. Competitive inhibition of xanthine oxidase, blocking the last two steps in uric acid biosynthesis"]','D','Allopurinol is a purine analog and xanthine oxidase inhibitor that reduces uric acid production by competitively inhibiting the last two steps in uric acid biosynthesis. Its active metabolite alloxanthine (oxypurinol) also inhibits xanthine oxidase with a half-life of 15-18 hours, allowing once-daily dosing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','A gout patient with an underexcreter phenotype and CrCl >50 mL/min wants a urate-lowering agent targeting the excretion problem. Which is most appropriate?','["A. Allopurinol \u2014 xanthine oxidase inhibitor reducing uric acid production", "B. Colchicine \u2014 anti-inflammatory preventing acute attacks", "C. Probenecid \u2014 uricosuric agent inhibiting URAT1 in the proximal tubule to increase uric acid excretion", "D. Febuxostat \u2014 non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor"]','C','Probenecid is a uricosuric agent that promotes renal clearance of uric acid by inhibiting the urate-anion exchanger (URAT1) in the proximal tubule, blocking reabsorption of uric acid. It is the appropriate agent for under-excreters with preserved renal function and is avoided if CrCl <50 mL/min.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','Which agent for chronic gout should NOT be started during an acute flare and should only be initiated after the attack resolves?','["A. Colchicine", "B. Probenecid (uricosuric agent \u2014 rapid urate changes can worsen acute attacks)", "C. Naproxen", "D. Prednisolone"]','B','Probenecid is a uricosuric agent indicated for long-term urate-lowering therapy. It should not be started during an acute gout flare as rapid changes in urate levels can precipitate additional attacks. Colchicine, NSAIDs, and corticosteroids are appropriate for acute management.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','What is the target serum urate level when treating chronic gout with urate-lowering therapy?','["A. Below 6 mg/dL to prevent deposition of urate crystals (saturation point)", "B. Below 3 mg/dL to dissolve all existing tophi within 3 months", "C. Below 9 mg/dL the normal upper limit of laboratory reference range", "D. Below 10 mg/dL as crystals only form at very high concentrations"]','A','Most therapeutic strategies for gout aim to lower serum uric acid below the saturation point of 6 mg/dL, thus preventing deposition of urate crystals. This target is necessary to gradually dissolve existing tophi and prevent new crystal formation and acute attacks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','How does febuxostat differ from allopurinol in structure, renal handling, and cardiovascular safety?','["A. Febuxostat is a purine analog like allopurinol with identical cardiovascular safety profile", "B. Febuxostat inhibits xanthine dehydrogenase only; allopurinol inhibits both xanthine oxidase and dehydrogenase", "C. Febuxostat requires significant dose reduction in all patients with renal impairment; allopurinol does not", "D. Febuxostat is a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor with less renal elimination and lower hypersensitivity risk but possibly increased cardiovascular risk versus allopurinol"]','D','Febuxostat is a non-purine oral xanthine oxidase inhibitor structurally unrelated to allopurinol. It has less renal elimination (less dose adjustment in reduced renal function) and a lower risk of rash/hypersensitivity. However, it should be used with caution in patients with a history of heart disease or stroke due to potentially greater cardiovascular risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','Why should initiation of urate-lowering therapy (e.g., allopurinol) be accompanied by prophylactic colchicine or NSAIDs for at least 6 months?','["A. Allopurinol requires anti-inflammatory co-therapy to achieve adequate urate lowering", "B. Prophylactic therapy prevents allopurinol-induced renal toxicity during dose titration", "C. Rapid changes in serum urate concentrations during treatment initiation can precipitate an acute gout attack", "D. Concurrent anti-inflammatory therapy prevents development of allopurinol resistance"]','C','Initiation of urate-lowering therapy can paradoxically precipitate an acute gout attack due to rapid changes in serum urate concentrations, which mobilise urate crystals from joints. Prophylactic anti-inflammatory therapy should be initiated with urate-lowering therapy and continued for at least 6 months.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','Which classic NSAID is considered the prototypical agent for treating acute gouty arthritis?','["A. Aspirin high-dose", "B. Indomethacin", "C. Celecoxib", "D. Diclofenac"]','B','Indomethacin is considered the classic NSAID of choice for acute gouty arthritis, although all NSAIDs are likely to be effective. Note: low-dose aspirin should be avoided in gout as it can inhibit uric acid excretion and exacerbate the condition.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Gout Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','Pegloticase is used for refractory gout. What is its mechanism, route, and key adverse effect requiring premedication?','["A. Purine analog inhibiting xanthine oxidase; oral daily dosing; Steven-Johnson syndrome requiring HLA screening", "B. Uricosuric agent inhibiting URAT1; oral twice daily; severe renal impairment", "C. Monoclonal antibody against IL-1; subcutaneous injection monthly; increased infection risk", "D. Recombinant uricase converting uric acid to allantoin; IV infusion every 2 weeks; infusion reactions and anaphylaxis requiring antihistamine and corticosteroid premedication"]','D','Pegloticase is recombinant urate oxidase (uricase) that converts uric acid to allantoin, a water-soluble nontoxic metabolite. It is administered as an IV infusion every 2 weeks for patients who fail standard therapies. Infusion reactions and anaphylaxis may occur, requiring premedication with antihistamines and corticosteroids.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','Which is the first-line pharmacological therapy for postmenopausal osteoporosis without contraindications?','["A. Bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate) \u2014 preferred first-line antiresorptive agents", "B. Calcitonin intranasal spray \u2014 preferred due to analgesic properties", "C. Raloxifene \u2014 preferred due to breast cancer prevention benefits", "D. Teriparatide \u2014 preferred due to anabolic mechanism"]','A','Bisphosphonates including alendronate, risedronate, and zoledronic acid are the preferred first-line agents for treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis. They decrease osteoclastic bone resorption, resulting in a small increase in bone mass and reduced fracture risk with several years of beneficial effects.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody used for osteoporosis. What is its target?','["A. Parathyroid hormone receptor \u2014 stimulating osteoblast activity", "B. RANKL \u2014 inhibiting osteoclast formation and function", "C. Estrogen receptor alpha \u2014 preventing post-menopausal bone loss", "D. Calcitonin receptor \u2014 inhibiting osteoclast activity"]','B','Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand (RANKL) and inhibits osteoclast formation and function. It is administered via subcutaneous injection every 6 months and is considered first-line particularly for patients at high fracture risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','How does teriparatide differ mechanistically from bisphosphonates in treating osteoporosis?','["A. Teriparatide inhibits bone resorption by blocking RANKL; bisphosphonates stimulate bone formation", "B. Teriparatide acts as a selective estrogen receptor modulator; bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite", "C. Teriparatide stimulates osteoblast activity to INCREASE bone formation (anabolic); bisphosphonates inhibit bone resorption", "D. Teriparatide inhibits osteoclast activity by binding hydroxyapatite in bone"]','C','Teriparatide is a recombinant form of human parathyroid hormone. Once-daily subcutaneous administration stimulates osteoblastic activity and increases bone formation and strength — an anabolic effect. In contrast, bisphosphonates, denosumab, and raloxifene inhibit bone resorption — an anti-resorptive mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','Why should teriparatide use be limited to no more than 2 years?','["A. Development of neutralising antibodies leads to complete loss of efficacy after 2 years", "B. Bone density gains plateau after 2 years making additional therapy futile", "C. Teriparatide causes severe hypercalcaemia which becomes life-threatening beyond 2 years", "D. Cumulative use beyond 2 years is not recommended due to osteosarcoma risk seen in animal (rat) studies"]','D','Both teriparatide and abaloparatide have been associated with an increased risk of osteosarcoma in rats. Although not established in humans, cumulative lifetime use for more than 2 years is not recommended as a precautionary measure. After completing therapy, an anti-resorptive agent should be started.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','What is the key administration instruction for oral bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate) to prevent esophageal injury?','["A. Remain upright for 30-60 minutes after taking and take on empty stomach with plain water only", "B. Take with orange juice to increase absorption", "C. Take after meals to minimise stomach upset", "D. Take at bedtime to allow prolonged gastric transit"]','A','To minimise esophageal irritation and risk of esophagitis/ulcers, patients should remain upright for 30 minutes after most bisphosphonates (60 minutes for ibandronate). They should be taken on an empty stomach with plain water only — food and other medications significantly impair the already poor (<1%) absorption.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','What is the absorption profile of oral bisphosphonates, and how should they be taken?','["A. Well absorbed (>80%) if taken with food to reduce GI irritation", "B. Poorly absorbed (<1%); take on empty stomach with plain water; food and medications significantly impair absorption", "C. Moderately absorbed (30-50%) regardless of food intake", "D. Absorbed via the lymphatic system, not affected by GI conditions or food"]','B','Absorption after oral bisphosphonate administration is poor (<1% of the dose). Food and other medications significantly interfere with absorption. Strict administration guidelines must be followed: empty stomach, plain water only, remain upright for 30-60 minutes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','Which bisphosphonate is administered intravenously once yearly, and what atypical fracture risk associated with long-term use may warrant a drug holiday?','["A. Ibandronate IV quarterly; osteonecrosis of the jaw is the primary long-term concern", "B. Pamidronate IV monthly; increased cardiovascular risk after 5 years", "C. Zoledronic acid IV annually; atypical femur fractures with long-term use (>5 years) may warrant drug holiday", "D. Clodronate IV bimonthly; renal failure with long-term use"]','C','Zoledronic acid is administered intravenously once per year for osteoporosis. Atypical femur fractures may occur with long-term bisphosphonate use, with risk increasing beyond 5 years of therapy. Guidelines recommend considering a drug holiday after 5 years of oral bisphosphonates or 3 years of zoledronic acid.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','medium','mcq','Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator. Which correctly describes its tissue-selective effects?','["A. Estrogen antagonist in bone (promotes resorption); estrogen agonist in breast and uterus", "B. Estrogen agonist in bone and breast; estrogen antagonist in uterus only", "C. Non-selective estrogen receptor agonist equivalent in effect to hormone replacement therapy", "D. Estrogen agonist in bone (preserves density); estrogen antagonist in breast and endometrium"]','D','Raloxifene has estrogen-like effects on bone (increases bone density) and estrogen antagonist effects on breast and endometrial tissue. It does NOT increase risk of endometrial cancer and decreases risk of invasive breast cancer. However, it has not been shown to reduce non-vertebral or hip fractures, unlike bisphosphonates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','easy','mcq','Which baseline intervention should be recommended for ALL patients at risk of or being treated for osteoporosis?','["A. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation", "B. Calcitonin + bisphosphonate combination therapy", "C. Raloxifene + teriparatide combination", "D. Hormone replacement therapy + bisphosphonate"]','A','Calcium and vitamin D supplementation is recommended for all patients at risk of or being treated for osteoporosis. Calcium carbonate (40% elemental calcium, taken with meals) or calcium citrate (21% elemental calcium, with or without food) are used. Vitamin D is essential for calcium absorption and bone health.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy','hard','mcq','A patient on long-term alendronate presents with jaw pain after a dental extraction that fails to heal. Which adverse effect of bisphosphonates does this represent?','["A. Atypical femoral fracture at the subtrochanteric region", "B. Osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) \u2014 exposed necrotic bone that fails to heal", "C. Fluorosis causing periosteal bone thickening and pain", "D. Sclerosing osteomyelitis from calcium precipitation in alveolar bone"]','B','Although uncommon, osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) may occur with bisphosphonate use, particularly following dental procedures. Risk factors include IV bisphosphonate therapy (especially in cancer patients), dental extractions, and poor oral hygiene. ONJ is characterised by exposed necrotic bone in the jaw that fails to heal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','medium','mcq','Baclofen is used as a muscle relaxant for spasticity. What is its mechanism of action?','["A. Direct acting on skeletal muscle blocking calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum", "B. Competitive antagonist at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction", "C. GABA-B receptor agonist at the spinal cord reducing motor neuron excitability by hyperpolarisation", "D. Centrally acting alpha-2 agonist reducing spinal interneuron firing"]','C','Baclofen is believed to affect GABA-B receptors at the level of the spinal cord. It is a GABA-B agonist that reduces spinal motor neuron excitability by hyperpolarising neurons, making it useful for treating spasticity from conditions such as multiple sclerosis and spinal cord injury.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','easy','mcq','Diazepam produces muscle relaxation at high doses. Via which mechanism?','["A. Blocks acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction reducing motor activity", "B. Directly inhibits skeletal muscle fibre actin-myosin interaction", "C. Blocks calcium channels in motor neurons preventing action potential propagation", "D. Enhances GABA-A receptor-mediated chloride influx, increasing presynaptic inhibition in the spinal cord"]','D','At high doses, benzodiazepines like diazepam relax spasticity of skeletal muscle by increasing presynaptic inhibition in the spinal cord, where alpha-2-GABA-A receptors are largely located. Diazepam is useful for skeletal muscle spasms and spasticity from multiple sclerosis and cerebral palsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','medium','mcq','Cyclobenzaprine is structurally related to which drug class, and what limits its use especially in elderly patients?','["A. Tricyclic antidepressants; anticholinergic side effects (dry mouth, urinary retention) and sedation", "B. Phenothiazines; extrapyramidal side effects and tardive dyskinesia", "C. Benzodiazepines; dependence, withdrawal, and tolerance", "D. Antihistamines; excessive sedation and paradoxical excitation"]','A','Cyclobenzaprine is structurally related to tricyclic antidepressants and acts centrally. It causes significant anticholinergic side effects (dry mouth, urinary retention, blurred vision) and sedation, limiting its use particularly in elderly patients. It is used for short-term relief of acute musculoskeletal spasm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','hard','mcq','Which muscle relaxant acts directly on skeletal muscle by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and what is its specific emergency indication?','["A. Baclofen \u2014 treatment of severe tetanus-induced muscle rigidity", "B. Dantrolene \u2014 treatment of malignant hyperthermia caused by volatile anaesthetic agents", "C. Diazepam \u2014 treatment of status epilepticus with concurrent muscle rigidity", "D. Cyclobenzaprine \u2014 treatment of neuroleptic malignant syndrome"]','B','Dantrolene acts directly on skeletal muscle by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, uncoupling excitation-contraction coupling. It is the specific treatment for malignant hyperthermia caused by volatile anaesthetic agents and is also used for chronic spasticity when central side effects are unacceptable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','easy','mcq','For which musculoskeletal condition is diazepam specifically indicated as a muscle relaxant?','["A. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with accessory muscle overuse", "B. Osteoporotic vertebral compression fractures", "C. Skeletal muscle spasms and spasticity from degenerative disorders such as multiple sclerosis and cerebral palsy", "D. Acute gouty arthritis with periarticular muscle guarding"]','C','Diazepam is specifically useful in the treatment of skeletal muscle spasms and in treating spasticity from degenerative disorders such as multiple sclerosis and cerebral palsy. The muscle relaxant effect is mediated by enhanced GABA-A receptor activity at the level of the spinal cord.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','medium','mcq','Tizanidine is a centrally acting muscle relaxant. What is its mechanism and a critical drug interaction?','["A. GABA-B agonist; baclofen combined with tizanidine causes additive respiratory depression", "B. Serotonin reuptake inhibitor; concurrent MAOIs cause serotonin syndrome", "C. Glycine agonist; benzodiazepines combined with tizanidine cause excessive CNS depression", "D. Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist; ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 dramatically increasing tizanidine levels causing hypotension and sedation"]','D','Tizanidine is a centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonist reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release at the spinal cord. A critical drug interaction: ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 (which metabolises tizanidine), dramatically increasing tizanidine plasma levels and causing potentially life-threatening hypotension and excessive sedation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','hard','mcq','A patient with MS has severe spasticity refractory to oral baclofen. What alternative delivery method provides direct spinal delivery with improved efficacy and reduced systemic side effects?','["A. Intrathecal baclofen via an implanted pump", "B. Intravenous baclofen infusion given in hospital", "C. Transdermal baclofen patch applied to the lower back", "D. Sublingual baclofen dissolving tablets"]','A','For severe spasticity refractory to oral baclofen, intrathecal baclofen delivered via an implanted pump provides direct delivery to the spinal cord, achieving higher concentrations at the target site with lower systemic doses and reduced systemic side effects such as sedation and generalized weakness.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','medium','mcq','Which benzodiazepine is most commonly used specifically for treating spasticity in multiple sclerosis and cerebral palsy?','["A. Alprazolam", "B. Diazepam", "C. Clonazepam", "D. Temazepam"]','B','Diazepam is specifically indicated for the treatment of skeletal muscle spasms and spasticity from degenerative disorders such as multiple sclerosis and cerebral palsy. Higher doses than those used for anxiety are required for the muscle relaxant effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','easy','mcq','Why is baclofen preferred over diazepam for long-term spasticity management in ambulatory patients with multiple sclerosis?','["A. Baclofen provides greater anti-inflammatory effects targeting underlying demyelination", "B. Baclofen is superior for treating acute muscle spasm from injury", "C. Baclofen causes less sedation and has lower addiction/dependence potential than diazepam", "D. Baclofen can be given intravenously for acute severe spasm in the emergency department"]','C','Baclofen is preferred for long-term spasticity management because it causes less sedation and has lower addiction/dependence potential compared with diazepam (a benzodiazepine). Preservation of cognitive function and lower dependence risk are crucial for ambulatory patients with chronic neurological conditions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Muscle Relaxants','hard','mcq','A patient taking tizanidine for MS-related spasticity is newly prescribed ciprofloxacin for a urinary tract infection. What is the expected drug interaction and management?','["A. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 causing dramatic increase in tizanidine levels \u2014 concurrent use is contraindicated", "B. Ciprofloxacin induces CYP3A4 reducing tizanidine levels, requiring dose increase", "C. Ciprofloxacin and tizanidine are renally excreted and compete for tubular secretion", "D. Ciprofloxacin blocks tizanidine''''s alpha-2 receptor binding via competitive inhibition"]','A','Ciprofloxacin is a potent inhibitor of CYP1A2, the main enzyme metabolising tizanidine. Concurrent use can increase tizanidine plasma levels up to 10-fold, causing severe hypotension, bradycardia, and excessive sedation. Concurrent use of tizanidine with ciprofloxacin or fluvoxamine is contraindicated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of local anaesthetics such as lidocaine for nerve blocks and joint injections?','["A. Activation of GABA-A receptors causing hyperpolarisation of nerve membranes", "B. Blockade of calcium channels at nerve terminals preventing neurotransmitter release", "C. Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase at peripheral sensory nerve terminals", "D. Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels preventing Na+ influx required for action potential propagation"]','D','Local anaesthetics block sodium ion channels to prevent the transient increase in permeability of the nerve membrane to Na+ required for action potentials. When propagation is prevented, sensation cannot be transmitted from the source of stimulation to the brain.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','hard','mcq','A patient develops local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) after a femoral nerve block for knee replacement. Which treatment is specifically indicated for LAST?','["A. Activated charcoal for GI decontamination", "B. Intravenous 20% lipid emulsion (lipid rescue therapy) \u2014 acts as a pharmacokinetic sink for lipophilic local anaesthetics", "C. High-dose intravenous naloxone to reverse CNS depression", "D. Intravenous magnesium sulphate for membrane stabilisation"]','B','Treatment for local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) includes seizure suppression, airway management, and cardiopulmonary support. Intravenous 20% lipid emulsion (lipid rescue therapy) is specifically indicated, as the lipid phase acts as a pharmacokinetic sink sequestering lipophilic local anaesthetics from cardiac and CNS tissue.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','medium','mcq','Why is epinephrine added to local anaesthetic preparations for joint injections and nerve blocks?','["A. Epinephrine directly enhances anaesthetic efficacy by acting on nociceptors", "B. Epinephrine counteracts histamine-mediated allergic reactions to the local anaesthetic", "C. Vasoconstriction decreases local absorption and systemic diffusion, prolonging duration of action and minimising toxicity", "D. Epinephrine reduces the pKa of the local anaesthetic, speeding onset of action"]','C','Local anaesthetics cause vasodilation leading to rapid diffusion away from the injection site. Adding epinephrine causes vasoconstriction, decreasing the rate of absorption and diffusion, minimising systemic toxicity, and increasing the duration of action by keeping the drug at the injection site longer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','medium','mcq','Which nerve fibres are MOST sensitive to blockade by local anaesthetics?','["A. Small unmyelinated C fibres and small myelinated A-delta fibres (pain, temperature, autonomic)", "B. Large myelinated A-alpha fibres (motor)", "C. Large myelinated A-beta fibres (proprioception, touch)", "D. All fibre types are equally sensitive to local anaesthetic blockade"]','A','Small, unmyelinated nerve fibres for pain, temperature, and autonomic activity are most sensitive to local anaesthetic blockade. This is clinically exploited in epidural anaesthesia where pain can be blocked at lower concentrations while preserving some motor function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','easy','mcq','Lidocaine belongs to which class of local anaesthetics, and what determines its metabolism?','["A. Ester class; hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase)", "B. Ester class; metabolised by hepatic CYP3A4", "C. Amide class; hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase", "D. Amide class; metabolised by hepatic enzymes"]','D','Lidocaine is an amide local anaesthetic. Biotransformation of amide local anaesthetics occurs primarily in the liver (hepatic enzymes). This contrasts with ester local anaesthetics (e.g., procaine), which are hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','hard','mcq','How does the pKa of a local anaesthetic affect its onset of action for nerve blocks in MSK procedures?','["A. Higher pKa = quicker onset \u2014 more drug in ionised form at physiologic pH for receptor binding", "B. Lower pKa = quicker onset \u2014 more drug in unionised form at physiologic pH, allowing faster membrane penetration", "C. pKa does not affect onset; lipid solubility is the only determinant of onset", "D. Lower pKa = slower onset \u2014 ionised form predominates, preventing membrane penetration"]','B','Local anaesthetics with a lower pKa have quicker onset, since more drug exists in the unionised form at physiologic pH (7.4), allowing better penetration of the nerve cell membrane. Once inside the membrane, the ionised form interacts with the protein receptor of the Na+ channel to inhibit its function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','medium','mcq','True allergy to amide local anaesthetics such as lidocaine is:','["A. Common, occurring in 5-10% of patients due to the aromatic ring structure", "B. Cross-reactive with ester local anaesthetics like procaine via para-aminobenzoic acid", "C. Exceedingly rare; allergy to one amide does not exclude use of another amide", "D. Always presenting with anaphylaxis requiring steroid premedication for subsequent use"]','C','True allergy to an amide local anaesthetic is exceedingly rare. Allergy to one amide does NOT rule out use of another amide (unlike esters where cross-reactivity occurs via the shared metabolite para-aminobenzoic acid). Many reported allergies are actually side effects from co-administered epinephrine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','medium','mcq','Why does local anaesthetic onset become delayed or prevented in infected tissues?','["A. Acidic pH of infected tissue increases the ionised drug form, reducing membrane penetration", "B. Bacteria produce esterases that degrade the local anaesthetic before it reaches the nerve", "C. Increased blood flow from inflammation washes away the local anaesthetic", "D. Bacterial toxins compete with local anaesthetics at sodium channel binding sites"]','A','The pH drops in infected tissue. At lower pH, more of the local anaesthetic exists in the ionised (protonated) form, which cannot penetrate the nerve cell membrane. Only the unionised form crosses the membrane, so reduced unionised fraction delays or prevents onset.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','easy','mcq','Which long-acting local anaesthetic is most commonly used for postoperative epidural analgesia after orthopaedic procedures?','["A. Lidocaine", "B. Procaine", "C. Mepivacaine", "D. Bupivacaine"]','D','Bupivacaine is one of the longest-acting local anaesthetics due to its high lipid solubility and protein binding. It is commonly used for epidural anaesthesia in obstetric and orthopaedic procedures. Its long duration makes it preferred for postoperative pain management, though it has higher cardiotoxicity than lidocaine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics in MSK','hard','mcq','A patient with pseudocholinesterase deficiency requires a digital nerve block for finger surgery. Which local anaesthetic carries increased toxicity risk, and why?','["A. Amide local anaesthetics (e.g., lidocaine) \u2014 hepatic metabolism is impaired by pseudocholinesterase deficiency", "B. All local anaesthetics \u2014 pseudocholinesterase deficiency universally impairs local anaesthetic clearance", "C. Ester local anaesthetics (e.g., procaine) \u2014 depend on plasma cholinesterase for hydrolysis; reduced activity causes drug accumulation", "D. Only local anaesthetics containing preservatives \u2014 pseudocholinesterase hydrolyses preservatives which then become toxic"]','C','Esters are biotransformed by plasma cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase). Patients with pseudocholinesterase deficiency metabolise ester local anaesthetics more slowly, leading to higher drug levels and potential toxicity. Amide local anaesthetics are metabolised by the liver and are safe in pseudocholinesterase deficiency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','medium','mcq','Which drug-induced musculoskeletal adverse effect can occur with statin therapy, and what is its most serious extreme?','["A. Myopathy/myalgia progressing to rhabdomyolysis \u2014 disintegration of skeletal muscle, potentially fatal", "B. Tendon rupture particularly of the Achilles tendon leading to avulsion", "C. Osteonecrosis of the jaw following dental procedures", "D. Steroid myopathy causing proximal limb-girdle weakness"]','A','Statin (HMG CoA reductase inhibitor) therapy can cause myopathy and myalgia, with the most serious extreme being rhabdomyolysis. Rhabdomyolysis involves disintegration of skeletal muscle and is rare but potentially fatal due to acute renal failure from myoglobinuria. CK levels should be measured in patients with muscle symptoms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','easy','mcq','Which antibiotic class carries an FDA black box warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture?','["A. Beta-lactams (e.g., amoxicillin, cefdinir)", "B. Macrolides (e.g., azithromycin, clarithromycin)", "C. Tetracyclines (e.g., doxycycline, minocycline)", "D. Fluoroquinolones (e.g., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin)"]','D','Fluoroquinolones carry boxed warnings for tendinitis, tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and CNS effects. The Achilles tendon is most commonly affected. Risk is increased in patients over 60, those on corticosteroids, and patients with kidney, heart, or lung transplants.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','medium','mcq','Which combination most significantly increases the risk of statin-induced rhabdomyolysis?','["A. Rosuvastatin combined with warfarin", "B. Simvastatin (CYP3A4 substrate) combined with azole antifungals or macrolide antibiotics", "C. Pravastatin combined with fibrates", "D. Atorvastatin combined with ACE inhibitors"]','B','Simvastatin is metabolised by CYP450 3A4. Inhibitors of this enzyme (azole antifungals, protease inhibitors, cyclosporine, erythromycin) increase statin plasma levels, significantly increasing risk of rhabdomyolysis. Gemfibrozil and niacin also increase risk of muscle adverse effects with statins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','hard','mcq','A 67-year-old patient on levofloxacin develops acute Achilles tendon rupture. Which combination of risk factors most significantly predisposed this patient?','["A. Female sex and history of previous tendon injury", "B. Renal impairment requiring dose reduction", "C. Age >60 years combined with concurrent corticosteroid use", "D. Concurrent NSAID use and physically active lifestyle"]','C','Risk factors for fluoroquinolone-induced tendinitis and tendon rupture include age >60 years, concurrent corticosteroid use, and solid organ transplantation (kidney, heart, lung). The combination of advanced age and corticosteroids represents the highest risk population. The mechanism may involve fluoroquinolone effects on collagen metabolism and tendon vascularity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','easy','mcq','Long-term corticosteroid use causing osteonecrosis (avascular necrosis) most commonly affects which joint?','["A. Femoral head (hip joint)", "B. Knee joint (distal femur or proximal tibia)", "C. Shoulder joint (glenohumeral)", "D. Ankle joint (talar dome)"]','A','Corticosteroid-induced osteonecrosis most commonly affects the femoral head, followed by the humeral head. Chronic corticosteroid use impairs bone vascularity leading to ischemic bone death. Risk correlates with dose and duration of corticosteroid therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','medium','mcq','Which genetic marker is strongly associated with severe allopurinol hypersensitivity (Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN) particularly in Asian populations?','["A. HLA-B*5701 (associated with abacavir hypersensitivity)", "B. CYP2C19 poor metaboliser genotype", "C. G6PD deficiency", "D. HLA-B*5801"]','D','Severe allopurinol hypersensitivity reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis) are strongly associated with the HLA-B*5801 allele, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean patients, and in patients with reduced renal function. Pre-treatment screening for HLA-B*5801 is recommended in high-risk Asian populations.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','hard','mcq','Which cellular mechanisms best explain statin-induced myopathy?','["A. Direct cytotoxicity from statin metabolites accumulating in skeletal muscle", "B. Depletion of coenzyme Q10 impairing mitochondrial function and reduced prenylation of proteins important for muscle cell survival", "C. Autoimmune T-cell mediated destruction of myofibrils", "D. Inflammatory myositis triggered by statin-induced complement activation"]','B','Statin-induced myopathy may involve depletion of mevalonate pathway products including coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone, essential for mitochondrial energy production) and reduction of prenylation of proteins important for muscle cell function. Risk factors include hypothyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, and genetic polymorphisms in SLCO1B1 (hepatic statin transporter).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','medium','mcq','Bisphosphonate use is associated with which two rare but serious musculoskeletal adverse effects?','["A. Rotator cuff tendinopathy and subacromial bursitis", "B. Drug-induced myositis and rhabdomyolysis", "C. Osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) and atypical femur fractures with long-term use", "D. Periostitis (fluorosis) and vertebral stress fractures"]','C','Although uncommon, osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures may occur with bisphosphonate use. The risk of atypical fractures increases with long-term use (>5 years). Current guidelines recommend considering a drug holiday after 5 years of oral bisphosphonates or 3 years of zoledronic acid.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pharmacology','Drug-Induced MSK Conditions','easy','mcq','Fluoroquinolones have direct effects on which two musculoskeletal structures, and why is their use limited in paediatric patients?','["A. Cartilage and tendons \u2014 arthropathy (arthralgia and arthritis) reported in paediatric patients limits use to specific scenarios", "B. Bone marrow and periosteum \u2014 causing aplastic anaemia and periostitis", "C. Synovium and bursa \u2014 causing synovitis and bursitis", "D. Ligaments and menisci \u2014 causing ligamentous laxity and meniscal degeneration"]','A','Fluoroquinolones can have effects on cartilage and tendons. Arthropathy (arthralgia and arthritis) is reported in paediatric patients because fluoroquinolones may damage developing cartilage. Use in the paediatric population is limited to specific clinical scenarios such as cystic fibrosis exacerbation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
